          %h1#Idea Idea
          %p
            The goal here is to define a category of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism"} cobordism
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+manifold"} Riemannian manifold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism+category"} cobordism category
            defines a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TQFT"} TQFT
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+cobordism"} Riemannian cobordism
            %em Euclidean quantum field theory
            \.
          %blockquote
            %p
              Notice however that the physicist’s use of the word “Euclidean” is different from the way Stolz-Teichner use it: for a physicist it means that the Riemannian structure is not
              = succeed "-Riemannian." do
                %em pseudo
              %em flat
              \.
          %p
            One central technical difference between plain topological cobordisms and those with Riemannian structure is that we want the functors on these to smoothly depend on variations of the Riemannian structure. This requires refining the bordism category to a
            %em smooth
            category. By the logic of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+and+quantity"} space and quantity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stack"} stack
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Diff"} Diff
            with values in categories. This realization will be described here.
          %h1#topological Part 1 (topological) bordism category
          %p
            %strong definition sketch
          %p
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Bord
                    %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bord_d
            has
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                as
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
                = succeed "-dimensional" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi d
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (d-1)
                %em smooth
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
            %li
              %p
                and the
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+space"} compact
                = succeed "-dimensional" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeomorphism"} diffeomorphism
                “rel boundaries” (i.e. those that restrict to the identy on the boundary)
          %p
            The composition of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
          %h1#Riemannian Part 2 Riemannian bordism category
          %p in all of the following
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the symbol
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
                denotes
                = succeed "-dimensional" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+manifold"} Riemannian manifold
              %p without boundary.
            %li
              %p
                %strong note on boundaries
                technically it is convenient to never ever work with manifolds with Riemannian or other structure with boundary. Instead, we always just mention manifolds without boundary and encoded the way in which they are still to be thouhgt of as
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism"} cobordism
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/collars"} collars
                into them. The manifolds with boundary could be obtained by cutting of at the
                %em core
                of these collars (see the definition below) but, while this is morally the idea, in the construction this is never explicitly considered.
              %p
                Also, later when we generalize
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supermanifold"} supermanifold
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %msub
                    %mi Bord
                    %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R Bord_d
            is defined using bicollars from the beginning
          %p
            an object in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %msub
                    %mi Bord
                    %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R Bord_d
            is a quintuple
          %p consisting of
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                a
                = succeed "-dimensional" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+manifold"} Riemannian manifold
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            %li
              %p
                a
                %strong core
                = succeed "-manifold" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi d
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (d-1)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Y
                        %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y^c
                sitting
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Y
                        %mi c
                      %mo ↪
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y^c \hookrightarrow Y
                in a thickening
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Y
                        %mi d
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y^d
                – being a
                = succeed "-manifold" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Y
                        %mo +
                      %mo ,
                      %msup
                        %mi Y
                        %mo −
                      %mo ↪
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y^+, Y^- \hookrightarrow Y
                two disjointly embedded open
                = succeed "-dimensional" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi Y
                            %mi c
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y^c
                    is in the closure of both
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi Y
                            %mo +
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y^+
                    and
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi Y
                            %mo −
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y^-
                %li
                  %p
                    that
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi Y
                            %mo +
                          %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                          %msup
                            %mi Y
                            %mo −
                          %mo =
                          %mi Y
                          %mo \
                          %msup
                            %mi Y
                            %mi C
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y^+ \coprod Y^- = Y \backslash Y^C
          %blockquote
            %p
              so the
              %em picture
              of an object, which is missing in this writeup here for the moment, is a
              = succeed "-dimensional" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d-1
          %p
            %strong definition
            A
            %strong Riemannian bordism
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi Y
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Y
                    %mn 0
                    %mi c
                  %mo ,
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Y
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ±
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Y_0,Y_0^c, Y_0^{\pm})
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi Y
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Y
                    %mn 1
                    %mi c
                  %mo ,
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Y
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ±
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Y_1,Y_1^c, Y_1^{\pm})
            is a triple
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi i
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi i
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\Sigma, i_0, i_1)
            where
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
                is a
                = succeed "-dimensional" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+manifold"} Riemannian manifold
                without boundary
            %li
              %p
                for
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i=0,1
                an open neighbourhood of the core
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi Y
                        %mi i
                        %mi c
                      %mo ↪
                      %msub
                        %mi W
                        %mi i
                      %mover
                        %mo ↪
                        %mi open
                      %msub
                        %mi Y
                        %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y_i^c \hookrightarrow W_i \stackrel{open}{\hookrightarrow} Y_i
              %p
                this defines the intersections
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi W
                        %mi k
                        %mo ±
                      %mo :
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi W
                        %mi k
                      %mo ∩
                      %msubsup
                        %mi Y
                        %mi k
                        %mo ±
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W^\pm_k := W_k \cap Y^\pm_k
                with the two collars for each
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mo =
                        %mn 0
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k = 0,1
            %li
              %p
                a smooth map
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi i
                        %mi k
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi W
                        %mi k
                      %mo →
                      %mi Σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_k : W_k \to \Sigma
              %p such that
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi i
                            %mi k
                          %mo :
                          %msubsup
                            %mi W
                            %mi k
                            %mo +
                          %mo ∪
                          %msubsup
                            %mi Y
                            %mi k
                            %mi c
                          %mo →
                          %mi Z
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_k : W^+_k \cup Y_k^c \to Z
                    is a proper map;
                %li
                  %p
                    (+) for
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msubsup
                            %mi i
                            %mi k
                            %mo +
                          %mo :
                          %mo =
                          %msub
                            %mi i
                            %mi k
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %msubsup
                            %mi W
                            %mi k
                            %mo +
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i^+_k := i_k/W^+_k
                    are
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isometry"} isometric
                    embeddings into
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi Σ
                          %mo \
                          %msub
                            %mi i
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msubsup
                            %mi W
                            %mn 1
                            %mo −
                          %mo ∪
                          %msubsup
                            %mi Y
                            %mn 1
                            %mi c
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma \backslash i_1(W^-_1 \cup Y^c_1)
                  %p i.e. restricted to the (+)-collar the embedding of the thickened object into the would-be cobordisms is isomertric
                %li
                  %p
                    the core
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi Σ
                            %mi c
                          %mo :
                          %mo =
                          %mi Σ
                          %mo \
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi i
                            %mn 0
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msubsup
                            %mi W
                            %mn 0
                            %mo +
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ∪
                          %msub
                            %mi i
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msubsup
                            %mi W
                            %mn 1
                            %mo −
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma^c := \Sigma \backslash (i_0(W^+_0) \cup i_1(W^-_1))
                    is compact
                  %p i.e. cutting of the (+)-collar of the incoming object and the (-)-collar of the outgoing object yields a compact manifold
          %p
            %strong> Remark
            \. Notice that this builds in an asymmetry: the (+)-side is preferred. This is intentionally: also the category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi TV
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} TV
            of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+vector+space"} topological vector space
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msup
                      %mi V
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \otimes V^*
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                  %msup
                    %mi Y
                    %mo *
                  %mo →
                  %mi ∅
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y \coprod Y^* \to \emptyset
            (where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Y
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y^*
            is obtained from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            by reversing orientation) but
            %em not
            one going the other way round.
          %p
            A big difference between
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TQFT"} TQFT
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TQFT"} TQFT
          %p
            %strong example
            Given any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isometry"} isometry
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi W
                    %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi W
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\phi : W_0 \to W_1
          %p
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            preserves the decomposition
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi W
                    %mi k
                    %mo ±
                  %mo ,
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Y
                    %mi k
                    %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W_k^\pm, Y_k^c
            we get a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+cobordism"} Riemannian cobordism
            using
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi W
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Sigma := W_1
          %p and
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi i
                    %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi Id
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi W
                        %mn 1
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi i
                    %mn 0
                  %mo =
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  i_1 = Id_{W_1}\,,\;\;\;\;\; i_0 = \phi
          %p
            %strong
              definition (morphisms in
              = succeed ")" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                      %msub
                        %mi Bord
                        %mi d
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R Bord_d
            morphisms from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Y
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y_0
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %msub
                    %mi Bord
                    %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R Bord_d
            (or
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo −
                  %mi RB
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d-RB
            or whatever the notation is) are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isometry"} isometry
            classes
            %em rel. boundary
            (see below) of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+cobordism"} Riemannian cobordism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Y
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y_0
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Y
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y_1
          %p We require the commutativity of the following diagram
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi V
                            %mn 1
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi i
                                %mn 1
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ←
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi i
                                %mn 0
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi V
                            %mn 0
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi f
                                %mn 1
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi f
                                %mn 0
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi V
                          %msub
                            %mo ′
                            %mn 1
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %mi i
                              %msub
                                %mo ′
                                %mn 1
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                          %mo ′
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ←
                            %mrow
                              %mi i
                              %msub
                                %mo ′
                                %mn 0
                        %mtd
                          %mi V
                          %msub
                            %mo ′
                            %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  V_1 &amp;\stackrel{i_1}{\to}&amp;
                  X
                  \&amp;\stackrel{i_0}{\leftarrow}&amp;
                  V_0
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow^{f_1} &amp;&amp;
                  \\downarrow &amp;&amp;
                  \\downarrow^{f_0}
                  \\\
                  V'_1 &amp;\stackrel{i'_1}{\to}&amp;
                  X'
                  \&amp;\stackrel{i'_0}{\leftarrow}&amp;
                  V'_0
                  }
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isometry"} isometry
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (F,f_0, f_1)
            is “rel. boundary” if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mn 0
                  %mo =
                  %mi Id
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_0 = Id
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %mi Id
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_1 = Id
          %p
            so an isomorphism “rel boundary” in the sense here (more “rel collars”, really) is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isometry"} isometry
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            sitting in a diagram
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi V
                            %mn 1
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi i
                                %mn 1
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ←
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi i
                                %mn 0
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi V
                            %mn 0
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi Id
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi Id
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi V
                          %msub
                            %mo ′
                            %mn 1
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %mi i
                              %msub
                                %mo ′
                                %mn 1
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                          %mo ′
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ←
                            %mrow
                              %mi i
                              %msub
                                %mo ′
                                %mn 0
                        %mtd
                          %mi V
                          %msub
                            %mo ′
                            %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  V_1 &amp;\stackrel{i_1}{\to}&amp;
                  X
                  \&amp;\stackrel{i_0}{\leftarrow}&amp;
                  V_0
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow^{Id} &amp;&amp;
                  \\downarrow &amp;&amp;
                  \\downarrow^{Id}
                  \\\
                  V'_1 &amp;\stackrel{i'_1}{\to}&amp;
                  X'
                  \&amp;\stackrel{i'_0}{\leftarrow}&amp;
                  V'_0
                  }
          %h2#onedim
            description for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d=1
          %p
            we decribe
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %msub
                    %mi Bord
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R Bord_1
            explicitly
          %p it has at least the object
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi pt
                  %mo =
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi pt
                              %mo −
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi pt
                              %mi c
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi pt
                              %mo +
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                            %mo −
                          %mtd
                            %mo •
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                            %mo −
                    %mo )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo ±
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  pt =
                  \\left(
                  \\array{
                  pt^- &amp; pt^c &amp; pt^+
                  \\\
                  \-- &amp; \bullet &amp; --
                  }
                  \\right)
                  \=
                  (\mathbb{R}, \{0\}, \mathbb{R}_\pm)
          %p
            which is a point with collar all of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
          %p
            %strong Lemma
            every object in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %msub
                    %mi Bord
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R Bord_1
            which is
            %em connected
            and not the empty set is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphic
            to this
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi pt
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} pt
          %p
            now for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \in \mathbb{R}_+
            consider the morphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi I
                    %mi t
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi R
                  %msub
                    %mi Bord
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi pt
                  %mo ,
                  %mi pt
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  I_t \in R Bord_1(pt,pt)
          %p
            defined as the triple
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi i
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi i
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathbb{R}, i_0, i_1)
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi i
                    %mn 0
                  %mo :
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_0 : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}
            is the identity map, and where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi i
                    %mn 1
                  %mo :
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_1 : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}
            is translation by
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
          %p
            This means that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi i
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_0
            takes the core of in the incoming point to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \in \mathbb{R}
            while
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi i
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_1
            takes the core of the outgoing point to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \in \mathbb{R}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            outside of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,1]
            is hence “collar” and this describes what naively one would think of as just the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval"} interval
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,1]
            regarded as a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+cobordism"} Riemannian cobordism
          %p
            %strong Lemma
            The composition of these cobordisms is given by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi I
                    %mi t
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mi I
                    %mrow
                      %mi t
                      %mo ′
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi I
                    %mrow
                      %mi t
                      %mo +
                      %mi t
                      %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  I_t \circ I_{t'} = I_{t+t'}
          %p There are also morphisms
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mo +
                  %mo :
                  %mi pt
                  %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                  %mi pt
                  %mo →
                  %mi ∅
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  L_+ : pt \coprod pt \to \emptyset
          %p and
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi R
                    %mo +
                  %mo :
                  %mi ∅
                  %mo →
                  %mi pt
                  %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                  %mi pt
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  R_+ : \emptyset \to pt \coprod pt
          %p
            which describe morally the same cobordisms as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi I
                    %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I_t
            does, but where both boundary components are regarded as incoming or noth as outgoing, respectively.
          %p
            Here
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_t
            is formall given exactly as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi I
                    %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I_t
            only that the map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi i
                    %mn 0
                  %mo :
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_0 : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}
            is not the identity, but reflection at the origin. This encodes the orientation reversal at that end.
          %p
            This is defined for
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo &gt;
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \gt 0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t= 0
            the morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_0
            is still defined, but
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi R
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R_0
            is not!! Exercise: check carefully with the above definition, keeping the asymmetry mentioned there in mind, to show that the obvious definition of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi R
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R_0
            does not satisfy the axioms above.
          %p
            So this means that we have a cobordism of length 0 going
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ∅
                    %mo →
                    %mi pt
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                    %mi pt
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \emptyset \to pt \coprod pt
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi pt
                  %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                  %mi pt
                  %mo →
                  %mi ∅
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} pt \coprod pt \to \emptyset
            will have to have non-vanishing length.
          %p
            Another morphism in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %msub
                    %mi Bord
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R Bord_1
            is the morphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                  %mo :
                  %mi pt
                  %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                  %mi pt
                  %mo →
                  %mi pt
                  %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                  %mi pt
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\sigma : pt \coprod pt \to pt \coprod pt
          %p which just interchanges the two points, without having any length.
          %p
            %strong Lemma
            We have the following composition laws:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi L
                        %mi t
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi σ
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi L
                        %mi t
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_t \circ \sigma = L_t
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi R
                        %mi t
                      %mo =
                      %mi σ
                      %mo ∘
                      %msub
                        %mi R
                        %mi t
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R_t = \sigma \circ R_t
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi R
                        %mi t
                      %msub
                        %mo ∘
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi L
                            %mn 0
                      %msub
                        %mi R
                        %mrow
                          %mi t
                          %mo ′
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi R
                        %mrow
                          %mi t
                          %mo +
                          %mi t
                          %mo ′
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R_t \circ_{L_0} R_{t'} = R_{t+t'}
          %p where in the last line we have the composition that is obvious once you draw the corresponding picture, which in full beuaty is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi Id
                    %mi pt
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msub
                    %mi Id
                    %mi pt
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∘
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi R
                    %mi t
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msub
                    %mi R
                    %mrow
                      %mi t
                      %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (Id_{pt} \otimes L_0 \otimes Id_{pt}) \circ (R_t \otimes R_{t'})
          %p
            where the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊗
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes
            is given by disjoint union.
          %p
            %strong theorem
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %msub
                    %mi Bord
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R Bord_1
            is
            %em generated
            as a symmetric monoidal category by
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the object
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi pt
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} pt
            %li
              %p
                the morphisms
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi L
                          %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_0
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %msub
                        %mi R
                        %mi t
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %mrow
                          %mi t
                          %mo &gt;
                          %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{R_t\}_{t \gt 0}
          %p subject to the relations
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi σ
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  L_0 \circ \sigma = L_0
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mi R
                    %mi t
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi R
                    %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\sigma \circ R_t = R_t
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∀
                  %mi t
                  %mo ,
                  %mi t
                  %mo ′
                  %mo &gt;
                  %mn 0
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi R
                    %mi t
                  %msub
                    %mo ∘
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi L
                        %mn 0
                  %msub
                    %mi R
                    %mrow
                      %mi t
                      %mo ′
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi R
                    %mrow
                      %mi t
                      %mo +
                      %mi t
                      %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\forall t,t' \gt 0 : R_t \circ_{L_0} R_{t'} = R_{t + t'}
          %p
            %strong corollary
            symmetric monoidal functors
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi Fun
                    %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %msub
                    %mi Bord
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi TV
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  E \in Fun^\otimes(R Bord_1, TV)
          %p
            to the category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi TV
                    %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} TV_\mathbb{R}
            of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+vector+space"} topological vector space
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                      %mo :
                      %mi pt
                      %mo ↦
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E : pt \mapsto V
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi L
                        %mn 0
                      %mo ↦
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi λ
                      %mo :
                      %mi V
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi V
                      %mo →
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E : L_0 \mapsto (\lambda :  V\otimes V \to \mathbb{R})
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi R
                        %mi t
                      %mo ↦
                      %msub
                        %mi ρ
                        %mi t
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi V
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E : R_t \mapsto \rho_t \in V \otimes V
          %p
            The map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                  %mo :
                  %mi V
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi V
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda : V \otimes V \to \mathbb{R}
            is necessarily a
            %em nondegenerate
            and
            %em symmetric
            bilinear form and thus may be used to produce and fix an isomorphism
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo ≃
                    %msup
                      %mi V
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \simeq V^*
          %p This isomorphism is used to get an embedding
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi V
                  %mi to
                  %mi V
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msup
                    %mi V
                    %mo *
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi End
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  V \otimes V to V \otimes V^* \hookrightarrow End(V)
                  \\,.
          %p The image of this embedding is the set of what in this context will be called “trace class” operators.
          %p
            With respect to this identification the map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
            is to be understood. For varying
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ρ
                    %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho_t
            form a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semigroup"} semigroup
            (for instance a typical example would be
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo =
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V = \Gamma(E)
            a space of sections of a vector bundle and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ρ
                    %mi t
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mi t
                      %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho_t = e^{-t \Delta}
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Laplace+operator"} Laplace operator
            on
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
          %p
            %strong note
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                  %mo :
                  %mi V
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi V
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda : V \otimes V \to \mathbb{R}
            to be continuous, one cannot use the Hilbert tensor product
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes_H
          %p the reason is that we have the folloing possible mpas out of the following possible tensor products
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mover
                    %mo ←
                    %mi λ
                  %mi V
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi algebraic
                  %mi V
                  %mover
                    %mo ↪
                    %mrow
                      %mi finite
                      %mi rank
                  %mi End
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbb{R}
                  \\stackrel{\lambda}{\leftarrow}
                  V \otimes_{algebraic} V \stackrel{finite rank}{\hookrightarrow}
                  End(V)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mover
                    %mo ←
                    %mi λ
                  %mi V
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi V
                  %mover
                    %mo ↪
                    %mrow
                      %mi trace
                      %mi class
                  %mi End
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbb{R}
                  \\stackrel{\lambda}{\leftarrow}
                  V \otimes V \stackrel{trace class}{\hookrightarrow}
                  End(V)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mover
                    %mo ←
                    %mi λ
                  %mi V
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi H
                  %mi V
                  %mover
                    %mo ↪
                    %mrow
                      %mi Hilbert
                      %mi Schmitdt
                  %mi End
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbb{R}
                  \\stackrel{\lambda}{\leftarrow}
                  V \otimes_H V \stackrel{Hilbert Schmitdt}{\hookrightarrow}
                  End(V)
          %p (so here the middle is the projective tensor product, the one that we are actually using)
          %h2#smoothversion smooth version / families version
          %p
            We now refine the definition of the categories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %msub
                    %mi Bord
                    %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R Bord_d
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi TV
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} TV
            such that they remember smooth stucture.
          %blockquote
            %p
              Effectively, what the following implicitly does is to refine these categories to
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stack"} stack
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Diff"} Diff
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibered+category"} fibred categories
              that appear in the following,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Bord
                      %mi d
                      %mi fam
                    %mo →
                    %mi Diff
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R Bord_d^{fam} \to Diff
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi TV
                      %mi fam
                    %mo →
                    %mi Diff
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} TV^{fam} \to Diff
              are the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+construction"} Grothendieck construction
              of these stacks.
          %p
            %strong
              definition of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi TV
                      %mi fam
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} TV^{fam}
          %p
            recall that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi TV
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} TV
            denotes the category of locally convex Hausdorff
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+vector+space"} topological vector space
          %p
            now let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi TV
                    %mi fam
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} TV^{fam}
            be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibred+category"} fibred category
            over
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Diff"} Diff
            whose fiber over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi Diff
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \in Diff
            is the category of topological
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundle"} vector bundle
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundle"} vector bundle
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+vector+space"} topological vector space
            = succeed "-morphisms" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty
          %p
            let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo ,
                  %mi W
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi TV
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V, W \in TV
          %p
            Then a linear map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %mi V
                  %mo →
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : V \to W
            is – for any inclusion
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \hookrightarrow V
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi V
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi f
                        %mtd
                          %mi W
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                            %mo ⊂
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↗
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  V &amp;\stackrel{f}{\to}&amp; W
                  \\\
                  \\uparrow^\subset &amp; \nearrow
                  \\\
                  U
                  }
          %p
            – called
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^1
            at
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi u
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u \in U
            in the direction
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi v
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v \in V
            if
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mi lim
                    %mrow
                      %mi t
                      %mo →
                      %mn 0
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi u
                      %mo +
                      %mi t
                      %mi v
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo −
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi u
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\lim_{t \to 0} \frac{F(u+t v) - F(u)}{t}
          %p
            exists in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            and
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ×
                  %mi V
                  %mo →
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  U \times V \to W
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi u
                  %mo ,
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi d
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi u
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (u,v) \mapsto d F_u(v)
          %p is continuous.
          %p
            Iteratively one defines
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^n
            and then
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty
            = succeed "-bundles" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi TV
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} TV
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty
            maps in this sense (linear in the fibers, of course)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi V
                          %mo ′
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mover
                              %mi f
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                        %mtd
                          %mi V
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi S
                          %mo ′
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi f
                        %mtd
                          %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  V' &amp;\stackrel{\tilde f}{\to}&amp; V
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow &amp;&amp;\downarrow
                  \\\
                  S' &amp;\stackrel{f}{\to}&amp; S
                  }
          %p
            %strong
              definition of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Bord
                      %mi d
                      %mi fam
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R Bord_d^{fam}
          %p
            Similarly
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Bord
                    %mi d
                    %mi fam
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R Bord_d^{fam}
            has as objects submersions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                  %mo →
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y \to S
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Y
                    %mi c
                  %mo →
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y^c \to S
            (not necessarily surjective) with a smooth rank-2 tensor on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            that fiberwise induces the structure of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+manifold"} Riemannian manifold
            (so these are
            = succeed "-families" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            = succeed "s)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+manifold"} Riemannian manifold
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Y
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo ←
                            %mo ⊂
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi Y
                            %mi c
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi submersion
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mo ↙
                            %mrow
                              %mi proper
                              %mi subm
                              %mo .
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  Y &amp;\leftarrow^\subset&amp; Y^c
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow^{submersion} &amp; \swarrow_{proper subm.}
                  \\\
                  S
                  }
          %p
            recall that a map is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proper+map"} proper map
            if inverse images of compact sets are compact.
          %p
            %strong remark
            Notice that if we fix the topology of the fibers in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y \to S
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+metric"} Riemannian metric
            on the fibers, so here each
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            can be thought of as a (subspace of a) moduli space of Riemannian metrics on a given topological space. Don’t confuse this with the role the space always called
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            here will play as a kind of “moduli space of field theories”.
          %p
            a morphism in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Bord
                    %mi d
                    %mi fam
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R Bord_d^{fam}
            in
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Bord
                    %mi d
                    %mi fam
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi Y
                              %mn 0
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi S
                              %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi Y
                              %mn 1
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi S
                              %mn 1
                    %mo )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  R Bord_d^{fam}\left(
                  \\array{
                  Y_0 \\ \downarrow \\ S_0
                  },
                  \\;\;
                  \\array{
                  Y_1 \\ \downarrow \\ S_1
                  }
                  \\right)
          %p
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isometry"} isometric
            rel boundary classes of submersions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma \to S_0
            such that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Σ
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ←
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi i
                                %mn 1
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi f
                            %mo *
                          %msub
                            %mi Y
                            %mn 1
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi Y
                            %mn 1
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi i
                                %mn 0
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi Y
                            %mn 0
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi S
                            %mn 0
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi f
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi S
                            %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\Sigma
                  \&amp;\stackrel{i_1}{\leftarrow}&amp;f^* Y_1 &amp;\to&amp;  Y_1
                  \\\
                  \\uparrow^{i_0} &amp;&amp;\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  Y_0&amp; \to&amp;S_0 &amp;\stackrel{f}{\to}&amp;
                  S_1
                  }
          %p
            so here
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            is an
            = succeed "-family" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_0
          %h2#fieldtheories Riemannian field theories
          %p
            %strong definition
          %p
            A
            %strong
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
              \-dimensional Riemannian quantum field theory
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+functor"} symmetric monoidal functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo ∈
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Fun
                    %mi Diff
                    %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Bord
                    %mi d
                    %mi fam
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi TV
                    %mi fam
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  E \in Fun^\otimes_{Diff}(R Bord_d^{fam}, TV^{fam})
          %p such that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi R
                          %msubsup
                            %mi Bord
                            %mi d
                            %mi fam
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi TV
                            %mi fam
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↘
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↙
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi Diff
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  R Bord_d^{fam} &amp;&amp;\stackrel{}{\to}&amp;&amp;
                  TV^{fam}
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \searrow &amp;&amp; \swarrow
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; Diff
                  }
          %p
            and such that it preserves
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
          %p
            (so its a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+functor"} cartesian functor
            between these
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibered+category"} fibered categories
            that is also symmetric monoidal)
          %h2#invertible_field_theories Invertible Field Theories
          %p
            In the study of invertible field theories, let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                  %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Bord
                    %mi n
                    %mi G
                  %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                  %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mid \mid Bord^G_n \mid \mid
            be the fundamental groupoid of the classifying space of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Bord
                      %mi n
                      %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bord^G_n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invertible+field+theory"} invertible field theory
            factors through this and the Picard subgroupoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}^*
            of the target.
          %p Kreck, S. Stolz, and P. Teichner.Invertible topological field theories are SKK invariants.
